  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nTuesday night through Thursday will continue to be on the dry side, \nthanks to an area of high pressure/subsidence that's progged to \ndevelop over the western Great Lakes, along with an upper-level \nridge over the Great Plains region. Temperatures will remain \nseasonably warm, with high temperatures forecast for Wednesday and \nThursday in the middle to upper 80s, and nighttime lows in the \nmiddle 60s.\n\nAs we go through the day Thursday, the aforementioned upper ridge \nbegins to break down, with a mid-level shortwave trough embedded in \nthe zonal flow. A quasi vertically-stacked upper low will accompany \nthe trough, which will help further support large-scale forcing over \nour region. Likely chances of showers and thunderstorms (50-70%) \nlook to increase late Thursday night into Friday. At this time, \nstrong to severe storm potential for this period appears to be \npretty low, given meager instability and deep-layer shear magnitudes \n(20-30 knots). The latest machine-learning severe probs from NSSL, \nNCAR, and CSU all show very small values, if anything. More likely, \nthis bout of precipitation appears to bring beneficial rainfall to \nthe area, with the latest NBM exceedance probabilities of a half \ninch of total rainfall through 12z/7 AM Saturday around 40 to 60% \nacross the area. With the precipitation and cooler air mass building \nin, temperatures look to be cool even further for the end of the \nweek, with highs warming to around 80 degrees for Friday and \nSaturday - a welcomed sign for those looking for relief from the \nheat.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: there could be some lingering chances of \nshowers/storms Saturday morning before the aforementioned stacked \nupper low moves out and another high pressure system builds in, \nbringing drier conditions to the area.",
